      "prompt_en": "COMPOSITION RULE, HIGHEST PRIORITY: the photograph fills the entire 16:9 frame edge to edge — never a letterbox band, never an empty panel, never a flat colour block. The bottom three-eighths of the image height — the lowest 37%, roughly the bottom 270 pixels of a 720-pixel-tall frame — contains nothing but dark wet asphalt running toward the camera, low in detail so overlay text stays readable on it. Every face, hand, fuel nozzle, can and sign sits in the upper five-eighths. The pump island and the car roofs run across the MIDDLE of the frame, never near the bottom edge. Frame the man from the chest up and place his head high, close to the top edge. A real fuel station on a provincial Russian road late at night, photographed from the middle of the road. The canopy fluorescent tubes are all lit and the plastic price panel is lit too, but every pump screen is dark and a strip of hand-written cardboard is taped around each nozzle handle. Yellow plastic jerrycans stand in a queue on the concrete where their owners left them, one folding camp chair among them. Down the right side of the frame a line of ordinary cars runs back into the dark until only headlights are left, engines off. In the upper right, framed from the chest up under the canopy light, a Russian man in his fifties in a worn quilted jacket holds an empty yellow jerrycan in one hand. Heavy upper eyelids, eyes following the queue away into the dark without focus, lips shut with one cheek drawn in, shoulders dropped, his free hand pushed into his pocket. Style: candid documentary photograph of a real place, not an advertisement, not CGI, not a composite. Shot on a full-frame camera with a 35mm lens at about f/2.8, standing eye level, available light only, true-to-life restrained colour, mild sensor noise, a slight handheld tilt, honest texture on skin, metal, plastic and wet concrete. Ordinary Russian roadside clutter belongs in the frame: cracked kerbstones, a dented bin, taped paper notices on the pump housing, scuffed painted lines, a tangled hose, stacked crates. Signs may clip a little but the overall image stays natural — never glowing, never hyper-saturated, no HDR look, no rim-light effects, no lens flare, no arrows, no circles, no cracks, no sparks, no graphic overlays of any kind. Background signs carry Cyrillic lettering as visual texture, small and partly out of focus; exact legibility is not required. 16:9. No title overlay text, no captions, no subtitle bars, no large lettering across the frame, no watermark, no logo."
