      "prompt_en": "COMPOSITION RULE, HIGHEST PRIORITY: the photograph fills the entire 16:9 frame edge to edge — never a letterbox band, never an empty panel, never a flat colour block. The bottom three-eighths of the image height — the lowest 37%, roughly the bottom 270 pixels of a 720-pixel-tall frame — contains nothing but the shadowed pavement running toward the camera, dark and low in detail so overlay text stays readable on it. Every face, hand, wooden case, car roof and window sits in the upper five-eighths. Frame the woman from the chest up and place her head high, close to the top edge. A wide Pyongyang avenue in the early evening, photographed from the pavement at ground eye level so that the convoy comes toward the camera. The left two-thirds is the road: a line of open trucks moving slowly past with plain wooden repatriation cases strapped in their beds, one soldier standing at the tailgate of each, and a scatter of people stopped along the kerb watching them. Between the trucks and the camera ordinary passers-by keep walking out toward the lens as if nothing were happening, and a freshly washed black imported sedan sits parked at the kerb with the evening reflected flat in its paint. The right third is separated by a lamppost and its shadow: there, larger than the rest and framed from the chest up, a North Korean woman in her sixties in a padded coat has lifted one hand a little and is counting the cases one at a time with her fingers. Eyes tracking the trucks, lips moving without sound, lower lids swollen, inner eyebrows lifted, her other hand closed tight on the handle of a cloth bag. Flat overcast evening light with the street lamps just coming on. Style: candid documentary photograph of a real place, not an advertisement, not CGI, not a composite. Shot on a full-frame camera with a 35mm lens at about f/2.8, ground eye level, available light only, true-to-life restrained colour, mild sensor noise, a slight handheld tilt, honest texture on skin, painted metal, wood and cloth. Ordinary street clutter belongs in the frame: kerb paint worn thin, a bicycle leaning on a pole, a dusty planter, overhead trolley wires, scuffed pavement slabs. The image stays natural — never glowing, never hyper-saturated, no HDR look, no rim-light effects, no lens flare, no arrows, no circles, no cracks, no sparks, no graphic overlays of any kind. Background signs carry Korean lettering as visual texture, small and partly out of focus; exact legibility is not required. 16:9. No title overlay text, no captions, no subtitle bars, no large lettering across the frame, no watermark, no logo."
